WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.035
-You have been banging the drum
on climate change,

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, more than
a lot of people have.

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you -- You know, you were
mentioning that, maybe,

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.573 size:75% position:38%
there's this sort of
generational shift.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:09.843
Do you feel like it's getting
through to older generations?

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:11.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like --
-Yeah.

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.145 line:92%
I think what you're seeing --

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're seeing a real change
in public opinion.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:15.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's two things
that are happening.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
One is,
I think the debate over,

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
"is the climate warming?"
is gone, basically.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:23.189
I mean, 15 of the hottest years
have been in the last 19 years.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, everyone gets it.
-Yeah.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There's still this sort of
ridiculous thing

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
about, "Well,
are humans causing it?"

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.796 size:75% position:38%
That's also receding
to the background.

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:31.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
The big question now
becomes one of prioritization.

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, there's lots of things
happening in the world.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.834
There are lots of things
happening in this country.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a lot of challenges
and issues we face.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:38.505 line:92%
And one of the key questions is,

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
do we understand
this is the priority?

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:42.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is this something
that we sort of structure

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
and orient American policy
around?

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:45.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was the case
in the New Deal,

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
meeting the challenge
of the Great Depression.

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:48.848 line:92%
It was the case of World War II.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was the case for multiple
decades for the Cold War.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, from administration
to administration,

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:57.190
the U.S. government both
domestically and internationally

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
oriented itself
around a singular challenge

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
which it saw
as defeating communism.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.596 line:92%
The question for us now is,

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:07.934
do we orient all of American
social policy, economic policy

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.235 line:92%
towards this challenge or not?

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm very happy
when you take time

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:12.672 line:92%
to talk about things like this.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, there was,
with the release

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the Barr memo
about the Mueller report --

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.944 size:84% position:42%
And, again, we're still
waiting for the report.

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:19.145 line:92%
But there was this criticism

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.414 size:75% position:38%
that came from a lot
of different angles

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.716 size:75% position:38%
that maybe the media
had focused too much

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
on what the Mueller report
was going to be

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:26.486 line:92%
and what it was going to do.

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:28.922
How do you -- Looking back at
the last two years, do you --

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you reflected back
on how you covered it all?

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:32.792 line:92%
-Yeah. I think we all have.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean,
the biggest thing to me is,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
a criminal and
counterintelligence
investigation

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the President of the
United States and his campaign

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:42.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
is big news in any universe
at any time.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-One that ends up leading

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
to his campaign manager
going to jail.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:48.675
And his personal lawyer
and fixer pleading guilty

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
to crimes is an enormous deal,
as well.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:53.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the big thing that has
driven this from the beginning

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:57.217
and my feeling about the Mueller
report from the beginning is,

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
there are many unanswered
factual questions

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:02.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
about what happened that
we need to know the answer to.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.022 line:92%
And the coverage was a way

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
of trying to get
to the bottom of it,

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:06.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
which we are still not
at the bottom of.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that's
entirely justified

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.730 line:92%
through any journalistic lens.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.465 line:92%
-I want to show one last photo.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:15.368
Because you've obviously been
in politics for a long time.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is this '04?
-That's '04, yeah.

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.038 line:92%
-And you were an organizer?

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I was like a canvasser,
field organizer

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:23.309
for an environmental group
that was knocking on doors

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.711 line:92%
in support of John Kerry.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:25.879 line:92%
-There we go. Look at that.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you walked up,
they were like,

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.216 line:92%
"Look at this bespectacled guy.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.050 size:78% position:39% line:92%
This bespectacled guy

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going talk to me about
the environment. I know it."

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:34.621
-He's deeply, deeply concerned.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:37.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were like,
"This guy's not with the NRA."

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.725 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What did you find
knocking on doors?

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, I loved
that experience.

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:46.199
I would knock on doors. I'd get
other people to knock on doors.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think one of
the great things about it is,

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we talk about politics
from 30,000 feet up

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:53.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
or you have a TV show,
you have to sort of simplify,

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:54.641 line:92%
put people in categories.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
People are so weird.
-Yeah.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:03:01.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Actual individual voters
are just irreducibly strange.

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the strangeness
of both people

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.052
and their political views
gets sort of smoothed out

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.086 line:92%
when you talk about categories.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:09.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, like,
"Oh, this demographic

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:09.789 line:92%
is going for this person."

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:12.792
But when you actually talk to
people, people have all sorts

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:14.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
of amazing combinations
of sensible ideas

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:19.098
combined with totally crazy
ideas right next to each other.

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.133 size:88% position:44% line:92%
And you sort of remember

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.402 size:88% position:44%
just how much of a human
enterprise democracy is.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:24.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's supposed to be nice.
-I mean, it is.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.338 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean,
it's terrifying because --

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's both terrifying
and inspiring.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.075
-Yeah, we all know a few humans
where you'd be like,

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:30.810 line:92%
"Oh, I'd rather not you, but..."

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I always appreciate
having you here.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:36.616
Congrats on six years.
-It's great to be here. Thanks.

